<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened launchd daemon for the Q-Periapt policy-agent reference service.

  This plist is the macOS OS-level isolation contract for `serve-agent`. It
  runs the daemon under a dedicated non-login service account with an
  owner-only umask and no core dumps; provision the `_qperiaptagent` role
  account, the 0700 service/configuration directories, and the binary at
  /opt/qperiapt/bin first.

  Install this file as /opt/qperiapt/launchd/com.qperiapt.policy-agent.plist,
  NOT under /Library/LaunchDaemons, and let com.qperiapt.policy-agent-rundir.plist
  load it. launchd creates the SockPathName node but never its parent
  directory, whose 0710 mode is the daemon's only enforced admission boundary,
  and that parent has to sit where nothing but root can rename it: on macOS
  /private/var/run is root:daemon 0775 without the sticky bit, so a gid-1
  process could rename a verified directory away and put its own, or a
  symlink, under launchd's bind. The socket therefore lives under the
  root-owned /opt/qperiapt/run instead. That job verifies every ancestor of
  the parent from / down, creates or adopts and verifies the parent itself,
  and only then runs launchctl bootstrap system on this file. Loaded from
  /Library/LaunchDaemons instead, this plist runs unordered against that job
  and can bind its socket before the boundary has been verified.

  Every /var path below is written canonically under /private/var. On macOS
  /var is a symlink to private/var, and the daemon opens each path component
  with O_NOFOLLOW, so a template written as /var/lib/... cannot start: the
  daemon refuses to traverse the symlink rather than silently resolving it.

  launchd offers no seccomp-equivalent syscall filter, and App Sandbox
  requires a signed bundle with entitlements, so the kernel-level syscall
  boundary documented for Linux does not exist here; the macOS boundary is
  the dedicated uid, the daemon's own descriptor-pinned 0700/0600 filesystem
  capabilities, and its extended-ACL rejection. Do not represent this plist
  as an App Sandbox or code-signing attestation.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.qperiapt.policy-agent</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/opt/qperiapt/bin/q-periapt-policy-agent</string>
        <string>serve-agent</string>
        <string>/opt/qperiapt/run/qperiapt-agent/agent.sock</string>
        <string>/private/var/lib/qperiapt-agent/state/agent.redb</string>
        <!-- REPLACE BOTH. Numeric IP:port only: these are parsed as a
             SocketAddr and a hostname is rejected at start.

             These placeholders are localhost, which is NOT the supported
             topology. README.md requires the witness and the instance-lease
             authority to live outside this host's rollback/restore domain; a
             restore of this host would otherwise roll back the witness and the
             lease authority with it, voiding the rollback protection they
             exist to provide. The Linux template enforces this by denying all
             egress until a drop-in names the real endpoints. launchd has no
             equivalent, so on macOS it rests on this instruction: point both at
             off-host addresses, or record the co-location as an accepted risk
             in the deployment evidence. -->
        <string>127.0.0.1:7841</string>
        <string>127.0.0.1:7842</string>
        <string>/private/var/lib/qperiapt-agent/config</string>
    </array>
    <!--
      launchd owns the listening socket: it creates it, applies the ownership and
      mode below, and removes it. The daemon adopts the descriptor published
      under the "agent" key and refuses to start if it is not given one; there is
      no self-bind fallback. The first ProgramArguments path above is the socket
      path the daemon requires the listener to be bound to, and it must equal
      SockPathName here.

      Caveats verified on this platform:
        * SockPathOwner/SockPathGroup/SockPathMode take NUMBERS, not names. The
          installer must resolve the daemon account and the transport group to a
          uid/gid and template this file, or pin the group to a known gid.
        * SockPathMode is decimal: 0660 is 432. Do not include file-type bits;
          they are discarded.
        * SockPathOwner and SockPathGroup are documented but have no field
          precedent: no daemon shipped with this OS uses either key. Verify the
          resulting socket's ownership on each target OS version rather than
          assuming it took effect.

      The daemon cannot check any of this: an AF_UNIX descriptor describes a
      socket object, not the filesystem node naming it, so fstat on the inherited
      descriptor reports a different inode and mode than the path. The enforced
      admission boundary is the parent directory, provisioned 0710 owned by the
      daemon account with the transport group, so only that group can traverse to
      the socket at all -- and every ancestor of that directory is a root-owned
      directory nobody else can write, so no other account can rename it out
      from under the path launchd binds.
    -->
    <key>Sockets</key>
    <dict>
        <key>agent</key>
        <dict>
            <key>SockPathName</key>
            <string>/opt/qperiapt/run/qperiapt-agent/agent.sock</string>
            <key>SockFamily</key>
            <string>Unix</string>
            <key>SockType</key>
            <string>stream</string>
            <!-- REPLACE BOTH. These are placeholders, not defaults: 0/0 is
                 root/wheel, which is neither the daemon account nor the
                 transport group. Left as shipped, the socket is owned by root
                 with mode 0660 and the daemon's own clients cannot reach it,
                 so this fails closed rather than open -- but it does fail.
                 Resolve the uid and gid at install time and template them. -->
            <key>SockPathOwner</key>
            <integer>0</integer><!-- uid of the daemon account -->
            <key>SockPathGroup</key>
            <integer>0</integer><!-- gid of the transport group -->
            <key>SockPathMode</key>
            <integer>432</integer><!-- 0660 -->
        </dict>
    </dict>
    <key>UserName</key>
    <string>_qperiaptagent</string>
    <key>GroupName</key>
    <string>_qperiaptagent</string>
    <key>InitGroups</key>
    <false/>
    <key>Umask</key>
    <integer>63</integer><!-- 0077 -->
    <key>SoftResourceLimits</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Core</key>
        <integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
    <key>HardResourceLimits</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Core</key>
        <integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>
    <!--
      How long launchd waits after SIGTERM before it sends SIGKILL. launchd's
      default is 20 seconds. The daemon observes the stop within one
      maintenance interval (one second), erases its in-process secrets, and
      releases the instance lease, and that release is a handful of bounded
      authority round trips of at most 5 seconds each -- up to four against
      an authority that accepts the connection and never answers, so about 20
      seconds worst case, right at the default. A SIGKILL there costs only a
      redb recovery at the next open, and the lease lapses at its TTL exactly
      as after a crash; 60 gives the release room to finish instead.
    -->
    <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
    <integer>60</integer>
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
